[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Designing Load, Stress, Endurance, and Spike Test Scenarios in Performance Testing"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"A robust performance testing strategy requires multiple scenario types to evaluate system behavior under different conditions. This tutorial covers the design principles and practical implementation of Load, Stress, Endurance, and Spike tests."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Load Testing Scenarios"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Load testing verifies that the application performs acceptably under anticipated peak user volumes. The goal is to identify bottlenecks, response time degradation, and throughput limits before deployment."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ramp-up: Gradually increase virtual users (VUs) to prevent artificial system shock."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Steady State: Maintain peak load for a sustained duration (e.g., 30 to 60 minutes) to observe resource leaks."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ramp-down: Gradually reduce VUs to zero to observe connection teardown and cleanup."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep } from 'k6';

export const options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '5m', target: 100 },
    { duration: '30m', target: 100 },
    { duration: '5m', target: 0 },
  ],
};

export default function () {
  http.get('https://test-api.k6.io/public/crocodiles/');
  sleep(1);
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Stress Testing Scenarios"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Stress testing pushes the application beyond normal operational capacity to determine its breaking point and how it recovers from failure. VUs are continuously increased until errors spike or the server crashes."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Identify maximum user capacity before system failure."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Observe graceful degradation versus catastrophic failure."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Verify automatic recovery mechanisms once load subsides."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Endurance (Soak) Testing Scenarios"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Endurance testing uncovers issues that only manifest over long periods, such as memory leaks, database connection pool exhaustion, or unreleased file descriptors. The system is subjected to a moderate, steady load for hours or days."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Set VUs to roughly 60-80 percent of peak expected load."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Run tests for 4, 8, 24 hours, or longer depending on the release cycle."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor infrastructure metrics like heap usage, garbage collection frequency, and disk I/O."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Spike Testing Scenarios"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spike testing evaluates system resilience under sudden, extreme surges in traffic, simulating events like flash sales or breaking news alerts. The workload jumps from zero or low traffic to massive peaks almost instantaneously."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Instantaneous or extremely fast ramp-up to peak VUs."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Short duration at peak load."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Rapid drop back to baseline to check auto-scaling behavior."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"export const options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '10s', target: 10 },
    { duration: '1m', target: 10 },
    { duration: '10s', target: 1000 },
    { duration: '3m', target: 1000 },
    { duration: '10s', target: 10 },
    { duration: '1m', target: 10 },
    { duration: '10s', target: 0 },
  ],
};"},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Designing effective performance scenarios requires aligning test objectives with business goals. By systematically combining Load, Stress, Endurance, and Spike tests, engineering teams can ensure high availability, resilience, and superior user experience under any condition."}]
